   "title": "Figure 1.1.3 · Raster and vector graphics enlarged",
   "alt": "Two panels. Left, raster: a small circle is enlarged eight times and turns into a grid of square pixels with a stepped, blocky edge. Labels: a grid of coloured pixels; used for photos such as JPEG and PNG; enlarged too far, edges turn blocky. Right, vector: the same small circle enlarged eight times stays perfectly smooth. Four anchor points sit on the curve with handles that control its shape. Labels: points joined by mathematical paths; used for logos and icons such as SVG; scales to any size without losing quality.",
   "caption": "Enlarge both and compare the edges: raster pixels become visible blocks, while a vector path is recalculated at every size and stays sharp.",
