   "screen": "Photography basics: exposure, focus and colour balance",
   "title": "Figure 3.7.1 · The exposure triangle and its side effects",
   "alt": "Left: a triangle with aperture, shutter speed and ISO at its corners and exposure, how bright the image is, in the middle. Right: three scales, each running from less light on the left to more light on the right. Aperture, the size of the lens opening: f/8 is a small opening with more of the scene in focus, good for a landscape; f/1.8 is a wide opening with a blurred background, a shallow depth of field, good for a portrait. Shutter speed, how long the sensor is exposed: 1/1000 second freezes motion, such as a cricket bowler; 1/15 second blurs motion, such as silky waterfall water, and needs a tripod. ISO, sensor sensitivity: ISO 100 is clean, for a sunny beach; ISO 1600 is brighter but adds noise or grain, for a night fete.",
   "caption": "Each setting lets in more light in a different way, and each has a side effect: depth of field, motion blur or noise. Change one and you may need to balance another.",
