   "screen": "Audio recording: microphones, acoustics, levels and noise",
   "title": "Figure 3.7.3 · Polar patterns and safe recording levels",
   "alt": "Top: three polar patterns seen from above, each around a microphone pointing up towards the sound source at the front. Cardioid: a heart shape that picks up from the front and rejects the back. Omnidirectional: a circle that picks up equally all around. Bidirectional: a figure of eight that picks up from the front and the back but not the sides. Bottom: a level meter in dBFS from minus 48 to 0. Around minus 40 is too low, because raising it later also raises noise. The target zone for speech peaks is minus 12 to minus 6 dBFS. From minus 6 to 0 is headroom. 0 dBFS is the maximum; anything that hits it clips and distorts permanently.",
   "caption": "Choose the pattern that hears your speaker and ignores the room. Then set levels so speech peaks land between -12 and -6 dBFS, well clear of 0.",
