   "screen": "Post-production workflow and file management",
   "title": "Figure 3.7.5 · The post-production workflow and a tidy project folder",
   "alt": "Left: a flowchart from top to bottom. Ingest: copy cards to the project drive and back up at once. Organise: bins, renamed clips and proxies. Sync: line up separate audio by waveform or clap. Assembly edit, rough cut, fine cut: story, then timing, then polish. Picture lock: no more changes to shot order or length. Sound mix, colour correction and grading, titles, captions and graphics. Review with the client or peers; a dashed feedback arrow labelled revise loops back to the sound, colour and titles stage. Export the master and platform versions, then archive. Right: an example folder named 2026-11_SportsDay_Promo containing 01_PreProduction, 02_Footage, 03_Audio, 04_Graphics, 05_Project_Files and 06_Exports. Below, a good file name, 2026-11-14_SportsDay_YouTube_v03.mp4, with a tick, and a bad one, final_FINAL2.mp4, with a cross.",
   "caption": "Follow the order so you never re-edit after colour and sound are done. Dates first, no spaces and version numbers keep files in order.",
